Per project conventions, all `vi.mock()` calls in test files must include `{ spy: true }` as an option. The existing mock of `'../helpers/mainConfigFile'` omits it. Add the option:
```ts
vi.mock('../helpers/mainConfigFile', { spy: true }, () => ({
  updateMainConfig: mocks.updateMainConfig,
}));
```
(or consult the project's Vitest version for the exact three-argument form). The sibling `rnstorybook-config.test.ts` demonstrates the `{ spy: true }` pattern.

The `prompt()` function returns a generic message that doesn't tell the user which specific addons will be moved. Comparable automigrations (e.g., `removeEssentials`) include the affected package names in the prompt so users can confirm the action before it runs. If the `Fix` type's `prompt` signature accepts the `result` object, consider using it here:
```ts
prompt({ result }) {
  const names = result.ondeviceAddons.map(getAddonName).join(', ');
  return `We detected on-device addons (${names}) in your \`addons\` config…`;
},
```
This gives users clear, actionable information in the CLI output.
